1
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:05,090
The interface is pretty simple.

2
00:00:05,090 --> 00:00:07,370
You put your horizontal "X" coordinate here,

3
00:00:07,370 --> 00:00:09,410
vertical "Y" coordinate here.

4
00:00:09,410 --> 00:00:11,740
When you're happy with those, you press this button.

5
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:13,930
Got it.

6
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:20,220
Leonard, you'll never guess who I just found online--

7
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:24,240
Nice shot.

8
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,630
Eh, his giant head did most of the work.

9
00:00:26,630 --> 00:00:28,740
Very mature.

10
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:31,250
You're lucky I'm out of Silly String.

11
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,070
As I was saying, Leonard,

12
00:00:33,070 --> 00:00:35,050
you'll never guess who I just found online:

13
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:36,550
Professor Proton.

14
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:37,770
You're kidding.

15
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:40,010
- He's still alive? - Yes!

16
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:41,370
Who's Professor Proton?

17
00:00:41,370 --> 00:00:43,650
He was the host of this great--

18
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:45,340
Yes!

19
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:47,280
Sorry, tell me about Professor Proton.

20
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:50,040
Professor Proton hosted my favorite science show

21
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,100
when I was a child.

22
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:52,550
I never missed an episode.

23
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:56,040
He demonstrated scientific principles using everyday objects.

24
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:57,360
It was pretty cool.

25
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:01,640
Aw, so cute when you use the word "Cool" wrong.

26
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:04,610
Like when kids say "pasghetti."

27
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:06,480
Oh, dear Lord!

28
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:07,430
Leonard, look!

29
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:10,260
He's still available for parties and events.

30
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:11,590
We should hire him.

31
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:13,210
Hire him to do what?

32
00:01:13,210 --> 00:01:14,910
Well, whatever we want.

33
00:01:14,910 --> 00:01:16,960
Hang out, do experiments,

34
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:18,350
make him take 12 pictures with us

35
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:20,580
so we can make a calendar.

36
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:22,870
It would be pretty awesome to hang out with him.

37
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:25,560
I just used "awesome" wrong, didn't I?

38
00:01:26,630 --> 00:01:28,980
Well, I'm E-mailing him right now.

39
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:30,340
Do you remember his old theme song?

40
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:31,700
Of course I do.

41
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:34,150
* Grab your goggles, put your lab coat on *

42
00:01:34,150 --> 00:01:36,930
* Here he comes, Professor Proton.*

43
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:06,800
Hey, I just found out I have to be at

44
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:08,300
the telescope lab all weekend.

45
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:11,120
Any chance you and Bernadette could take care of my dog?

46
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:12,790
Why don't you put her in a kennel?

47
00:02:12,790 --> 00:02:15,480
Why don't you put your mother in a home?

48
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:21,110
To be honest, she'd do better in the kennel.

49
00:02:22,630 --> 00:02:24,840
I'll talk to Bernie. I'm sure it's fine.

50
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:25,500
Thank you.

51
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:28,430
It's happening! It's happening!

52
00:02:28,430 --> 00:02:30,560
Professor Proton is coming to our house.

53
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:31,880
You're kidding!

54
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:35,070
You mean the guy who used to host that lame kids show?

55
00:02:35,070 --> 00:02:38,000
And you just got yourself uninvited.

56
00:02:38,490 --> 00:02:40,910
See? I told you I'd find a tactful way to do that.

57
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:43,580
How'd you get him to come to your house?

58
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:45,700
As Professor Proton always says,

59
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:47,950
"There is no problem you can't solve

60
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:50,380
- if you use your noggin." - And he wrote him a check.

61
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:52,410
Yeah, that, too. Big check.

62
00:02:54,850 --> 00:02:57,420
Uncle Howard!

63
00:03:01,890 --> 00:03:05,940
Cinnamon's here for her sleepover party!

64
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:10,260
You know if you had a stroke, she'd eat you, right?

65
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:14,220
And it would be my pleasure to be her num-num.

66
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:19,940
Okay, so what do I need to know to take care of her?

67
00:03:19,940 --> 00:03:21,210
It's very simple.

68
00:03:21,210 --> 00:03:23,720
For breakfast, she has an egg-white frittata.

69
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:24,840
Feel free to give her a choice

70
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:27,300
of home fries or an English muffin, but not both.

71
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:29,770
We're watching our weight.

72
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:32,050
Uh, for dinner, something simple--

73
00:03:32,050 --> 00:03:34,970
a veal chop, some scampi, whatever you like.

74
00:03:35,990 --> 00:03:37,920
- Classy dog. - Yes.

75
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,100
Also, don't forget to close the toilet

76
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:41,320
or she'll drink out of it.

77
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:43,670
I feel for ya.

78
00:03:43,670 --> 00:03:47,220
I've got a psychotic mommy, too.

79
00:03:51,830 --> 00:03:54,130
Leonard, are you in bed?

80
00:03:56,130 --> 00:04:01,020
- Yes. - Me, too.

81
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:03,250
Great.

82
00:04:03,250 --> 00:04:05,820
I can't sleep.

83
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:09,140
Well, I can, so shut up.

84
00:04:10,150 --> 00:04:12,120
Do you realize that in less than nine hours,

85
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:15,060
Arthur Jeffries, a.k.a. Professor Proton,

86
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:16,630
will be in our apartment?

87
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:18,850
Sheldon, you know that if you stay up all night,

88
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:20,410
you're gonna be sleepy tomorrow.

89
00:04:20,410 --> 00:04:22,610
And a sleepy Sheldon is a cranky Sheldon.

90
00:04:22,610 --> 00:04:24,050
And a cranky Sheldon...

91
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:25,890
is actually no different than a regular Sheldon.

92
00:04:25,890 --> 00:04:27,830
Good night!

93
00:04:31,470 --> 00:04:34,460
I'm thinking of wearing a tuxedo.

94
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:37,700
That's not ridiculous. Good night.

95
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:42,980
Do you have cufflinks?

96
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:45,630
- No. - Ah, just as well.

97
00:04:45,630 --> 00:04:48,440
Where can you rent a tuxedo 3:00 in the morning?

98
00:04:49,050 --> 00:04:51,530
Okay, good night.

99
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:56,670
Nice to meet you, Professor Proton.

100
00:04:57,760 --> 00:05:00,460
Nice to meet you, Professor Proton.

101
00:05:05,110 --> 00:05:08,290
Nice to meet you, Professor Proton.

102
00:05:08,290 --> 00:05:10,130
Nice to meet you, Prof...

103
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:33,320
Leonard?

104
00:05:35,370 --> 00:05:37,460
What?

105
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:39,440
I still can't sleep.

106
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:44,940
She really tuckered herself out at the park, huh?

107
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:47,470
Yeah, you two were so cute playing together.

108
00:05:47,470 --> 00:05:49,080
It was kind of fun throwing a ball

109
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:51,880
and not having anyone laugh at me.

110
00:05:52,630 --> 00:05:54,160
And you were sweet not to throw it too far

111
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:57,000
so she didn't wear out her tiny legs.

112
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,730
Yeah, that's what I was doing.

113
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:03,060
You know, there were a few moments today

114
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:05,300
when I almost felt like we were a little family.

115
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:06,670
- Really? - Yeah.

116
00:06:06,670 --> 00:06:08,190
I never thought of myself as a mom,

117
00:06:08,190 --> 00:06:09,750
but when the three of us were out there having fun,

118
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:12,110
I felt like maybe someday we could do it.

119
00:06:12,110 --> 00:06:14,290
Of course we can.

120
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:18,560
Especially if our baby's as calm and quiet

121
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:20,330
as little Cinna...

122
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:23,080
Son of a bitch, she's gone!

123
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:24,280
Where'd she go?!

124
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:26,760
I don't know! She didn't leave a note!

125
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:28,970
You were the one who was supposed

126
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:30,370
to put her back in the stroller!

127
00:06:30,370 --> 00:06:31,480
No, I wasn't! You were!

128
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:32,860
- No, I wasn't! - Yes, you were!

129
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:35,600
Yeah, well, you throw like a girl!

130
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:45,300
I'm getting worried.

131
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:48,480
Relax, Sheldon, he's only a few minutes late.

132
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:51,600
Professor Proton was never late when he was on TV.

133
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:54,730
You know, every day, 4:00, he was there.

134
00:06:55,180 --> 00:06:57,100
Unless tornadoes were ripping apart East Texas,

135
00:06:57,100 --> 00:06:59,600
in which case, we'd join him in progress.

136
00:07:03,650 --> 00:07:05,830
It's him.

137
00:07:08,090 --> 00:07:10,220
Hello.

138
00:07:10,710 --> 00:07:12,780
Well, I see.

139
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:14,640
Yes.

140
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:16,860
All right, we can come get you.

141
00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:18,280
Yeah, well, see you soon.

142
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:20,150
- Bye. - Where is he?

143
00:07:20,150 --> 00:07:21,720
The third floor landing.

144
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:24,710
The poor old guy's been walking up the stairs for half an hour.

145
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:30,900
It's really you.

146
00:07:35,610 --> 00:07:37,700
Mr. Jeffries, I am so sorry.

147
00:07:37,700 --> 00:07:40,030
We should've told you about the broken elevator.

148
00:07:40,040 --> 00:07:42,060
I agree.

149
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:47,070
Professor Proton, it's an honor to meet you.

150
00:07:47,070 --> 00:07:49,280
Just-just call me Arthur.

151
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:51,280
Leonard...

152
00:07:52,870 --> 00:07:54,170
...you hear that?

153
00:07:54,170 --> 00:07:57,010
Professor Proton said I should call him Arthur.

154
00:07:57,010 --> 00:07:59,270
That means we're friends.

155
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:00,640
No.

156
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:04,900
A friend would've-would've told me about the elevator.

157
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:09,630
Look at me.

158
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:13,200
I can get as close to you as I want

159
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:16,130
without my mom saying it's going to ruin my eyes.

160
00:08:19,580 --> 00:08:22,360
Is, uh, is he dangerous?

161
00:08:23,810 --> 00:08:26,300
Actually, he's a genius.

162
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:28,030
I am.

163
00:08:29,170 --> 00:08:32,900
Th-That doesn't answer my question.

164
00:08:33,410 --> 00:08:35,410
Mr. Jeffries, I'm-I'm Leonard.

165
00:08:35,410 --> 00:08:36,880
This is my girlfriend, Penny.

166
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:39,430
- Hi. - Hello.

167
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:44,750
Well, I hope I haven't kept

168
00:08:44,750 --> 00:08:47,590
the kids waiting too long for-for the show.

169
00:08:47,590 --> 00:08:49,340
Oh, no, there are no kids.

170
00:08:49,340 --> 00:08:51,520
No, the-the show's for me.

171
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:54,440
Come on. I'll race ya...

172
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:57,040
Arthur.

173
00:09:00,950 --> 00:09:04,300
Is the, is the blonde girl really your-your girlfriend?

174
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:05,530
Yes, sir.

175
00:09:05,530 --> 00:09:07,770
You're the genius.

176
00:09:24,810 --> 00:09:27,740
So, do you do a lot of appearances like this?

177
00:09:27,740 --> 00:09:29,280
It-It's hard to say.

178
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:31,840
I'm-I'm still trying to figure out what...

179
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:35,870
what-what this is.

180
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:39,150
We just wanted to hang out with you

181
00:09:39,150 --> 00:09:41,600
and maybe learn a little about your life.

182
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:44,810
Well, there-there really isn't too much to tell.

183
00:09:44,810 --> 00:09:47,350
After the TV show was-was canceled,

184
00:09:47,350 --> 00:09:49,650
nobody in the scientific world

185
00:09:49,650 --> 00:09:52,460
would, uh, would take me seriously.

186
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:55,300
So I was forced to do these,

187
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:59,410
uh, children's parties to-to make a living.

188
00:09:59,410 --> 00:10:00,470
That's too bad.

189
00:10:00,470 --> 00:10:03,900
But still, working with kids-- it must be rewarding.

190
00:10:03,900 --> 00:10:06,800
You-you get bit a lot.

191
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:11,400
Let me see if-if I have this straight.

192
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:14,750
You-you two are-are physicists,

193
00:10:15,410 --> 00:10:17,530
and-and you, and you want me

194
00:10:17,530 --> 00:10:22,360
to do a children's... science show?

195
00:10:22,970 --> 00:10:24,220
Yes.

196
00:10:25,020 --> 00:10:26,430
And if there's time,

197
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:29,530
take 12 pictures with us in seasonal clothing.

198
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:37,950
You know, I'm a real scientist.

199
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:41,980
I-I have a PhD from Cornell University.

200
00:10:41,980 --> 00:10:42,740
Yeah, that's great.

201
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:44,650
Did you bring your puppet?

202
00:10:45,710 --> 00:10:48,310
No, no.

203
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:52,370
I-I hate that puppet.

204
00:10:52,370 --> 00:10:54,520
Oh, no.

205
00:10:54,520 --> 00:10:56,630
How could anybody hate

206
00:10:56,630 --> 00:10:59,880
Gino the Neutrino?

207
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:01,480
It's nice, huh?

208
00:11:01,480 --> 00:11:03,320
I got him for 20 bucks on eBay.

209
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:05,890
Including a-shipping!

210
00:11:07,740 --> 00:11:11,150
I'm... I'm awake, right?

211
00:11:15,090 --> 00:11:17,230
Th-This is happening?

212
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:20,950
Cinnamon!

213
00:11:20,950 --> 00:11:22,770
Cinnamon!

214
00:11:22,770 --> 00:11:24,610
You know, maybe she doesn't recognize her name

215
00:11:24,610 --> 00:11:26,690
because of Raj's accent.

216
00:11:27,940 --> 00:11:28,910
Good thinking.

217
00:11:28,910 --> 00:11:32,150
Cinnamon, come to Daddy!

218
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:34,650
Cinnamon!

219
00:11:34,650 --> 00:11:37,370
Where are you, my little lamb chop?

220
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:40,690
- Nice. - Thanks.

221
00:11:40,690 --> 00:11:43,430
When this all blows over, remember that voice.

222
00:11:43,430 --> 00:11:45,640
It's kind of a turn-on.

223
00:11:47,290 --> 00:11:49,500
It turns you on when I sound like Raj?

224
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:55,080
Cinnamon!

225
00:11:56,870 --> 00:12:01,100
Okay, as-as I put the egg on top,

226
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:03,510
and-and the flame goes out

227
00:12:03,510 --> 00:12:07,680
and-and the air pressure decreases in-in the flask,

228
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:11,010
what do you think will happen?

229
00:12:11,010 --> 00:12:12,030
I think I know.

230
00:12:12,030 --> 00:12:13,870
It's gonna get sucked in.

231
00:12:14,990 --> 00:12:17,180
It's going to get sucked in.

232
00:12:17,180 --> 00:12:19,940
Okay, I didn't know.

233
00:12:23,850 --> 00:12:25,370
Yes!

234
00:12:26,940 --> 00:12:29,270
See, I'm not a scientist like them.

235
00:12:30,300 --> 00:12:32,590
I-I figured that out.

236
00:12:33,720 --> 00:12:36,020
Uh, potato clock. Do potato clock.

237
00:12:36,020 --> 00:12:37,330
What's that?

238
00:12:37,330 --> 00:12:40,970
I-I power a clock with a, with a potato.

239
00:12:40,970 --> 00:12:42,200
Shut up!

240
00:12:42,210 --> 00:12:44,220
You can do that?

241
00:12:46,290 --> 00:12:47,590
I mean...

242
00:12:47,590 --> 00:12:50,550
wouldn't that solve the world's energy crisis?

243
00:12:52,870 --> 00:12:55,080
No.

244
00:12:57,190 --> 00:12:58,200
Look, guys,

245
00:12:58,200 --> 00:13:00,110
keep your money. I-I think, uh...

246
00:13:00,110 --> 00:13:01,880
I-I think I'm done.

247
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:03,460
What-what's wrong?

248
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:04,690
Is she upsetting you?

249
00:13:04,690 --> 00:13:06,820
Because I can make her go away.

250
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:12,000
No, sh-she's the only reason I've-I've stayed this long.

251
00:13:13,650 --> 00:13:15,430
Then what is it?

252
00:13:15,430 --> 00:13:16,360
I don't know.

253
00:13:16,360 --> 00:13:18,260
I think I'm just...

254
00:13:18,260 --> 00:13:20,670
I-I just, I just don't want to be

255
00:13:20,670 --> 00:13:23,460
Professor Proton anymore.

256
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:26,010
Well, how can you say that?

257
00:13:26,010 --> 00:13:28,830
Professor Proton's the best.

258
00:13:28,830 --> 00:13:31,360
What-what has it ever gotten me?

259
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:33,620
I mean, I'm-I'm an 83-year-old man who has...

260
00:13:33,620 --> 00:13:37,910
potatoes in-in his suitcase.

261
00:13:38,590 --> 00:13:41,560
Other scientists think-think I'm a joke.

262
00:13:41,560 --> 00:13:42,580
And the...

263
00:13:42,580 --> 00:13:47,300
the puppeteer who did, who did Gino...

264
00:13:47,300 --> 00:13:50,970
well, he also...

265
00:13:51,830 --> 00:13:54,000
did my wife.

266
00:13:58,580 --> 00:14:00,220
Mr. Jeffries,

267
00:14:00,220 --> 00:14:02,780
I need to show you something.

268
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:07,250
I'm sorry to hear about your troubles.

269
00:14:07,250 --> 00:14:08,510
Uh, thanks.

270
00:14:08,510 --> 00:14:10,970
But if you don't mind me asking,

271
00:14:10,970 --> 00:14:13,880
the potato clock, how does it work?

272
00:14:14,710 --> 00:14:18,170
Is it a trick clock or a trick potato?

273
00:14:22,710 --> 00:14:26,390
Wh-What do you two talk about?

274
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:36,290
I wrote a fan letter to you when I was a child in Texas,

275
00:14:36,290 --> 00:14:40,450
and you sent this autographed picture back to me.

276
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:42,790
Do you remember that?

277
00:14:43,780 --> 00:14:44,990
I'll-I'll give you a hint:

278
00:14:44,990 --> 00:14:49,580
I have a bracelet with my own address on it.

279
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:53,320
Well, anyway, um,

280
00:14:53,320 --> 00:14:55,100
you may find this hard to believe,

281
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:58,530
but I didn't have any friends growing up.

282
00:14:59,560 --> 00:15:01,730
No, I-I get that.

283
00:15:03,890 --> 00:15:07,540
But, um, I did have you.

284
00:15:07,540 --> 00:15:09,420
And every day at 4:00,

285
00:15:09,420 --> 00:15:12,190
you'd come to my house on Channel 68,

286
00:15:12,190 --> 00:15:14,710
and we'd do science together.

287
00:15:14,710 --> 00:15:16,710
If it hadn't been for you,

288
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:18,580
well, who knows what would've become of me?

289
00:15:18,580 --> 00:15:21,170
You know? Instead of a world-class physicist,

290
00:15:21,170 --> 00:15:24,440
I could've wound up as a-a hobo.

291
00:15:24,950 --> 00:15:27,130
Or a surgeon.

292
00:15:28,140 --> 00:15:31,360
I bet there are important discoveries being made every day

293
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:35,290
because you inspired millions of kids to pursue science.

294
00:15:35,290 --> 00:15:37,840
In a way, their discoveries are your discoveries.

295
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:38,770
Yeah, it's true.

296
00:15:38,770 --> 00:15:40,990
A generation of young scientists

297
00:15:40,990 --> 00:15:43,240
are standing on your shoulders.

298
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:45,690
Well, thank-thank you, guys.

299
00:15:45,690 --> 00:15:49,560
That... that-that means a lot.

300
00:15:49,560 --> 00:15:52,900
Well, it's important you know how much you mean to us.

301
00:15:54,150 --> 00:15:56,150
Arthur, are you okay?

302
00:15:56,150 --> 00:15:59,430
I'm having a...

303
00:15:59,440 --> 00:16:02,040
problem with my pacemaker.

304
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:03,740
I'll-I'll call for help.

305
00:16:03,740 --> 00:16:06,660
Any chance we could plug it into the potato?

306
00:16:11,350 --> 00:16:13,380
No.

307
00:16:24,460 --> 00:16:26,050
Hello?

308
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:28,910
What do you mean, you found my dog?

309
00:16:28,910 --> 00:16:31,170
She's with my friends.

310
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:34,130
Is she okay?

311
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:35,350
Oh, thank you.

312
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:38,080
Uh, just text me your address; I'm on my way.

313
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:39,650
Oh, and if she's hungry, go ahead and feed her.

314
00:16:39,650 --> 00:16:41,840
But do not give her anything starchy.

315
00:16:41,840 --> 00:16:43,860
She's having risotto for dinner.

316
00:16:47,050 --> 00:16:48,100
Your vitals are stable,

317
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:50,330
but let's take you in for some tests just to be safe.

318
00:16:50,330 --> 00:16:52,220
You want one of us to go with you in the ambulance?

319
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:53,820
I'll do it!

320
00:16:54,990 --> 00:16:57,490
He's not a relative, he's not allowed, right?

321
00:16:57,490 --> 00:16:58,930
No, that's not a rule. He can go.

322
00:16:58,930 --> 00:17:00,210
Oh, yeah!

323
00:17:01,690 --> 00:17:03,580
I can't catch a break today.

324
00:17:05,140 --> 00:17:07,250
We'll pack up your stuff and meet you at the hospital.

325
00:17:07,250 --> 00:17:09,280
I'm sorry things turned out this way.

326
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:11,740
Well, at-at this point, I'm just...

327
00:17:11,740 --> 00:17:15,060
glad someone's carrying me down the stairs.

328
00:17:19,380 --> 00:17:21,040
Met my childhood hero,

329
00:17:21,040 --> 00:17:23,070
now I get to ride in an ambulance.

330
00:17:23,070 --> 00:17:24,740
Boy, if we can get him to do that calendar,

331
00:17:24,740 --> 00:17:26,590
this'll be the best day ever.

332
00:17:30,270 --> 00:17:32,250
Can't believe we lost her.

333
00:17:32,250 --> 00:17:33,250
What was I thinking?

334
00:17:33,250 --> 00:17:35,210
I'd be a terrible mom.

335
00:17:35,210 --> 00:17:36,930
Well, maybe with the first one.

336
00:17:36,930 --> 00:17:38,230
But kids are like pancakes.

337
00:17:38,230 --> 00:17:40,330
The first one's always a throwaway.

338
00:17:44,510 --> 00:17:46,310
- How's this look? - It's fine.

339
00:17:46,860 --> 00:17:48,230
Where'd you get that picture of her?

340
00:17:48,230 --> 00:17:48,860
It's not her.

341
00:17:48,860 --> 00:17:51,380
I just Googled "Foo-foo little dogs."

342
00:17:53,650 --> 00:17:56,160
It's Raj. Stay quiet.

343
00:17:58,560 --> 00:17:59,530
Bad timing.

344
00:17:59,530 --> 00:18:02,150
Bernadette just took Cinnamon out for a walk.

345
00:18:02,150 --> 00:18:03,820
Interesting.

346
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:07,000
Did they take a walk down Liars' Lane?

347
00:18:07,650 --> 00:18:08,560
What?

348
00:18:08,560 --> 00:18:10,220
A lane frequented by liars.

349
00:18:10,220 --> 00:18:12,490
Like you, you big liar!

350
00:18:14,210 --> 00:18:15,500
You have her?

351
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:17,490
Oh, thank God she's okay.

352
00:18:17,490 --> 00:18:19,420
Well, I trusted you, and you let me down.

353
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:21,960
The poor thing's been shaking for hours.

354
00:18:21,960 --> 00:18:23,300
I'm really sorry.

355
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:25,330
Hang on, you've had her for hours?

356
00:18:25,330 --> 00:18:26,740
Yes. I picked her up,

357
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:29,400
and then we both went for massages to try and calm down.

358
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:31,340
And then we got Pinkberry.

359
00:18:33,020 --> 00:18:34,420
So you knew she was okay,

360
00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:36,560
and you couldn't pick up the phone to tell us?

361
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:38,090
Well, I-I thought about...

362
00:18:38,090 --> 00:18:39,710
Don't "well" me, mister!

363
00:18:39,710 --> 00:18:41,050
We've been worried sick!

364
00:18:41,050 --> 00:18:43,090
She could have been dead for all we knew!

365
00:18:43,090 --> 00:18:45,210
You should be ashamed of yourself!

366
00:18:45,210 --> 00:18:46,360
Sorry. I-I just...

367
00:18:46,360 --> 00:18:47,450
Sorry's not good enough!

368
00:18:47,450 --> 00:18:49,020
Maybe you need to take some time

369
00:18:49,020 --> 00:18:50,760
and think about what you've done!

370
00:18:55,900 --> 00:18:57,100
Nice guilt trip.

371
00:18:57,100 --> 00:19:00,530
You are gonna be an amazing mom.

372
00:19:08,140 --> 00:19:11,360
* Soft kitty, warm kitty, *

373
00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:14,940
* little ball of fur. *

374
00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:19,810
* Happy kitty, sleepy kitty, *

375
00:19:19,810 --> 00:19:23,210
* purr, purr, purr. *

376
00:19:23,210 --> 00:19:24,050
Thank you, Sheldon.

377
00:19:24,050 --> 00:19:25,800
That-that was very nice.

378
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:27,760
- Want me to sing it again? - No.

379
00:19:30,790 --> 00:19:33,830
The fourth, the fourth time was-was the charm.

380
00:19:34,930 --> 00:19:36,680
There anything I can get for you?

381
00:19:36,680 --> 00:19:38,680
Some apple juice?

382
00:19:38,680 --> 00:19:39,800
Uh, some Jell-O?

383
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:40,980
No. No, thank you.

384
00:19:40,980 --> 00:19:42,350
But I-I do...

385
00:19:42,350 --> 00:19:44,800
I do have a favor to-to ask.

386
00:19:44,800 --> 00:19:46,000
Name it.

387
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:47,670
Well, I'm-I'm booked to do

388
00:19:47,670 --> 00:19:49,580
a children's party tomorrow,

389
00:19:49,580 --> 00:19:53,520
and, um, frankly I, you know, I-I don't feel up to it.

390
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:55,180
Oh, you're not.

391
00:19:55,180 --> 00:19:57,200
You look awful.

392
00:19:59,620 --> 00:20:01,410
Th-Thank you.

393
00:20:02,930 --> 00:20:04,680
Anyway, uh...

394
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:08,070
I mean, you know my act better than... anybody.

395
00:20:08,070 --> 00:20:09,180
I was, I was hoping

396
00:20:09,180 --> 00:20:10,520
that maybe, you know,

397
00:20:10,520 --> 00:20:12,190
maybe you'd fill in for me.

398
00:20:12,190 --> 00:20:13,840
Wha... Are you saying

399
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:17,050
that you want me to be Professor Proton?

400
00:20:17,050 --> 00:20:18,250
Yeah.

401
00:20:18,250 --> 00:20:20,360
Oh, my.

402
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:21,700
What an honor.

403
00:20:21,700 --> 00:20:24,860
Oh, this is like being asked to ascend Mount Olympus

404
00:20:24,860 --> 00:20:26,920
and dine with the gods.

405
00:20:28,240 --> 00:20:33,090
Or a... Korean family in Alhambra.

406
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:36,720
But they'll know I'm not you.

407
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,950
Should I call myself...

408
00:20:38,950 --> 00:20:41,710
Professor Proton, Jr.?

409
00:20:41,710 --> 00:20:43,730
Sounds great.

410
00:20:43,730 --> 00:20:47,150
So, in a way...

411
00:20:47,150 --> 00:20:49,640
it's like I'm your son.

412
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:52,210
What-Whatever.

413
00:20:55,520 --> 00:20:57,720
Father.

414
00:21:00,750 --> 00:21:02,770
Sure, what the hell?

